// memory.mn — Memory and Alloc effect declarations
//
// These are Mentl's substrate effects. The emitter recognizes each
// operation by name and lowers it directly to a WASM instruction.
// No handler is written in Mentl for these — the WASM runtime IS
// the handler. But the declarations exist here so:
//
//   1. The type checker knows the signatures.
//   2. The developer can read what operations exist.
//   3. They participate in the graph like every other effect.
//   4. Effect rows are honest: `Memory` in a row means the function reads
//      linear memory, `MemoryWrite` that it writes it, and `ImageSwap` that
//      it replaces the whole image. Arithmetic on a word's bits (`i32_xor`
//      and its six siblings) is a primitive and performs nothing.
//
// This is the single source of truth for substrate operations.
// If an operation isn't declared here, it doesn't exist.
//
// Memory, MemoryWrite, ImageSwap: reads of linear memory, writes into it,
// and the swap of the whole image. Each op maps to one instruction or one
// emitted routine.
// Cast — a value's address. A heap value's word IS the address of its
// record ("a handle IS a word"), and addr answers it as one, typed, so it
// stays an address wherever it goes: an arena's exit that finds one of its
// region's addresses keeps the region rather than reclaim what it names.
// The way back from an address to a structured value is str_of_buf alone,
// the one localized coercion. Capability-gated by the row: `with Cast`
// admits it and a declared `!Cast` severs it transitively, the user-effect
// class. Lower erases the perform to its operand (identity at the word
// level, zero emission), so the row is the entire point.
effect Cast {
  addr(a) -> Addr
}

effect Memory {
  // 32-bit load — the full i32 word at an address
  //
  // (addr) -> value
  load_i32(Addr) -> Int
  // 8-bit load — the single byte at an address
  //
  // (addr) -> byte (0-255)
  load_i8(Addr) -> Int
  // An address is the word that names memory: what an allocation answers,
  // what a load reads from and a store writes to. It is a word in every
  // machine respect and not a number — it moves by addr_at, never by
  // arithmetic, and orders by magnitude — and its type is what an arena's
  // exit reads: an address of its region found where the exit looks (its
  // value, a field, an element, a slot its barriers journaled) keeps the
  // region whole, since nothing can say what the address names. The
  // guarantee is about values: a word computed from an address is the
  // program's own claim, and bits copied by mem_copy are opaque — written
  // into older memory, they keep the arena.
  //
  // (addr, bytes) -> the address that many bytes on
  addr_at(Addr, Int) -> Addr
  // (a, b) -> the bytes from b to a
  addr_diff(Addr, Addr) -> Int
  // (addr) -> the address stored there
  load_addr(Addr) -> Addr
  // The address no allocation answers, which a virgin slot reads.
  null_addr() -> Addr
  // How many 64 KiB pages the memory holds now, so a program can read its
  // own footprint and a fixture can state the pages a module's start needs.
  memory_pages() -> Int
  // An address's word, and the address a word names: the puns where a value
  // crosses the runtime's word protocol (a list slot holds its element as a
  // word). Each is the identity on the machine.
  addr_word(Addr) -> Int
  word_addr(Int) -> Addr
  // The live world — the top of the install chain ($world_g; emit projects
  // the global read, the heap_mark shape). 0 = the empty world. A Memory
  // read, not an Alloc verb: the perform path reads it on every dispatch.
  world_top() -> Int
  // The fork boundary's world reset — the fork pair's THIRD leg (graph
  // checkpoint + heap region + world). Writes $world_g back to a value
  // world_top() returned at the fork point. NOT a general setter: its one
  // sound use is the fork boundary, where the restored value predates
  // every push the forked extent made (the chain nodes above it die with
  // the region). Everywhere else the world's writers stay the emitted
  // pushes and bracket restores.
  world_restore(Int)
  // 64-bit float load, the wide-value boundary (§5.U): a wide value
  // crosses the word altitude by reference (its address is a word, a handle
  // is a word), and this op reads its native width at a typed site, 1:1
  // to f64.load. `store_f64` is its pair in `MemoryWrite`.
  //
  // (addr) -> value
  load_f64(Addr) -> Float
  // The image [0, heap-line) plus the mutable globals IS the whole program
  // state (wasm call frames live engine-side, outside linear memory), so a
  // persisted process is one contiguous copy + one globals record — no
  // serializer, no relocation: a same-build restore re-enters the exact
  // address space. The emitter owns the census (it declared every mutable
  // global), so the save/restore pair and the build key are PROJECTIONS of
  // the module over itself, emitted beside $alloc.
  //
  // image_key — this build's identity word: a hash of the fn table (indices),
  // the interned data (offsets), and the mutable-global census (record
  // layout) — exactly the three same-build-only word classes an image copy
  // depends on. Equal keys ⟺ the image's baked words resolve identically.
  image_key() -> Int
  // globals_area — the globals record's byte size (8 bytes per global).
  globals_area() -> Int
}

// MemoryWrite: a write into linear memory through an address. Every op
// writes the memory at its first argument, so the judgment reads what a
// store reaches off the op's effect, and `!MemoryWrite` says a function
// never writes memory through an address. Building a value is `Alloc`:
// filling a record nothing else holds yet is part of making it. A store
// into memory older than the frame making it also performs Mutate, which
// the judgment charges where it sees the address's age.
effect MemoryWrite {
  // (addr, value) — the full i32 word at an address
  store_i32(Addr, Int)
  // (addr, byte) — the single byte at an address
  store_i8(Addr, Int)
  // (addr, value) — an address into a slot; journaled where the slot is
  // older than an open arena
  store_addr(Addr, Addr)
  // (addr, value) — the wide-value pair of `load_f64`, 1:1 to f64.store
  store_f64(Addr, Float)
  // Bulk memory copy — src to dst, n bytes, as bits. Bits carry no type,
  // so a copy that writes memory older than an open arena keeps that
  // arena's region, and no copied byte is ever read as an address; a store
  // that says what it stores is how a program keeps an arena reclaiming.
  //
  // (dst, src, n)
  mem_copy(Addr, Addr, Int)
  // globals_save — write every mutable global to an 8-byte-slot record at
  // dst. Slot 0 is the heap line (the persist packer patches it to the
  // image extent so restore rewinds allocation to the persisted moment).
  globals_save(Addr)
}

// ImageSwap: the swap of the whole image, which writes every byte of memory
// and so reaches every value at once.
effect ImageSwap {
  // image_restore — stage the wire's globals+image above both heaps, copy
  // the image down over [0, size), restore the globals, return the wire's
  // named continuation address. Raw preamble choreography (no allocation
  // may interleave; the caller's heap values die under it — read
  // everything you need into locals first).
  image_restore(Addr) -> Addr
}

// The arenas' census: how many exits ran, how many kept their region, the
// KB reclaimed and moved, the highest line an exit reached (KB), and how
// many arenas a suspended computation left open — what the emitter reads
// where an arena lives, and zero where none does.
fn arena_stat(s: ArenaStat) = 0

type ArenaStat = ArExits | ArKept | ArFreed | ArMoved | ArHigh | ArSuspended

// Alloc — heap allocation. Bump allocator in WASM linear memory.
// alloc(n) advances the heap pointer by n bytes (8-byte aligned)
// and returns the old pointer. No free. No GC. Region-based
// lifetime via ownership effects.
//
// Alloc is separate from Memory because a function can read/write
// existing memory without allocating (with Memory + !Alloc), and
// this distinction is load-bearing for real-time audio paths where
// allocation is forbidden.
effect Alloc {
  // (size) -> the address of the fresh bytes
  alloc(Int) -> Addr
  // Where the heap line stands: the cost lines measure from it, and an arena
  // opens at it. There is no rewind beside it — reclaiming an extent is the
  // arena's (lib/arena.mn), which keeps what the extent published and zeroes
  // the memory behind it, so no reclaim depends on its caller knowing that
  // nothing older points into what it drops. In a module that spawns, each
  // instance allocates in a run of its own and this reads the IMAGE's
  // frontier past every run, which is what a persisted image must hold; an
  // arena reads its own instance's line in the runtime.
  heap_mark() -> Addr
}

// Mutate — a store into memory older than the frame that makes it: a
// parameter's memory, a capture, a module value, a feedback line. `!Mutate`
// proves a function leaves all memory that existed before it ran as it found
// it. No operations: the judgment charges it wherever a store's target is older.
effect Mutate {}

// Footprint — the memory a program claims it fits in, in bytes, written on
// main: `fn main() with Footprint(2359296) = …`. The author writes the
// number and the compiler sizes the module by it. The module declares its
// memory's maximum as the 64 KB pages that cover the claim, so the
// allocator stops the program rather than grow past it, and an arena's
// journal takes a quarter of the claim, at most 2 MB and at least the 8 KB
// an arena needs to keep anything. A claim smaller than what the program's
// start already needs (its static data and what the module's start
// function allocates before main runs) is refused when the program is
// compiled or checked, and the refusal says what the start needs. No
// operations: nothing performs it.
effect Footprint(bytes: Int) {}

// PARTIALITY IS A ROW FACT. The substrate traps a partial primitive whose
// precondition fails — an integer division by zero, INT_MIN / -1, an index
// past its sequence — and a site whose precondition the judgment cannot
// prove performs Trap, so `with !Trap` claims a function reaches no trap it
// has not bounded. Its one operation stops the machine where it is
// performed: the root answers it (no handler is written in Mentl, as for
// Memory) with the stop's said line, `trap: a precondition failed at` the
// perform's own address, it never returns, and nothing intercepts it — its
// absence is the point. A function that never returns is not a trap: that
// is `Diverge`, below. The op never returns, and says so: `!` is quantified
// per perform, so a deliberate trap stands where a value of any type is
// owed — the complement arm of a match over a word the representation
// bounds, `_ => trap()`, with no value fabricated after it.
effect Trap {
  trap() -> !
}

// Diverge — a computation that may never finish. A function that calls
// itself performs it unless every such call shrinks one of its parameters,
// handing each earlier shrinking parameter back unchanged: a counter that
// climbs by a constant toward a bound the calls keep, one that falls by a
// constant toward a floor, one divided toward zero, a part of the value the
// parameter matched (a constructor's field or a list pattern's rest), or a
// link the parameter's list node holds. A call into a cycle of two or more
// functions, and a function handed on as a value inside its own cycle,
// perform it too. So `!Diverge` claims a function always finishes, and the
// judgment checks the claim through every function it calls. No
// operations: the judgment charges it where a recursion has no measure.
effect Diverge {}

// The live world's identity: the install chain from world_top() down, hashed
// over each node's effect key and its handler's slot-0 arm word — a table
// index, so two handlers of one effect differ and two installs of one handler
// agree whatever state they hold. A persisted image carries the world that
// wrote it (handler = state: the graph's own handlers return with the graph,
// and the persisting verb's output sink with them), so it may resume only
// into that world; the driver files warm images under this key. A world is
// the word of its chain's top node, so the walk reads it as the address it
// names.
fn world_key() = world_key_from(word_addr(world_top()), -2128831035)

fn world_key_from(n, h) = if n == null_addr() {
  h
} else {
  let entry = load_addr(addr_at(n, 4))
  let record = load_addr(entry)
  let arm =
    load_i32(addr_at(record, 8 + 4 * (load_i32(addr_at(record, 4)) + load_i32(addr_at(entry, 4)))))
  world_key_from(
    load_addr(addr_at(n, 8)),
    i32_xor(i32_xor(h, load_i32(n)) * 16777619, arm) * 16777619
  )
}

// Pure — the empty effect row. Pure is not an effect with operations
// — it IS the absence of effects. `with Pure` means "this function
// performs no effects."
//
// In the row algebra:
//   Pure = EfRow([], [], EtClosed)  — the empty closed triple
//
// The type checker special-cases `Pure` as syntactic sugar for the
// empty closed row. No declaration needed — it's a keyword, not
// an effect. Documented here for completeness.
//
// fn add(a, b) with Pure = a + b
//   → performs nothing; row is the empty closed triple; type checker enforces
//     that the body contains no perform statements.
// The live install chain — [key@0][entry@4][parent@8][install_world@12]
// nodes consed by every install's $world_push — is walked from world_top()
// by interned-key i32.eq (string constants dedup — W5 — so the key the
// install PLACED and the key the perform SEEKS are the SAME offset), and
// the perform dispatches through the FOUND NODE: node.entry is the same
// [record][base] view the install built; node.install_world is the world
// OUTSIDE that install, which the dispatch sets around the arm call (the
// deep-handler law, delivered dynamically — an arm's own performs resolve
// outer, never self). A computation therefore performs in the world where
// it is CALLED, not the world where its closure was minted — the mint-time
// evidence snapshot (the retired keyed frame regions) was a cache of this
// dynamic fact. A perform miss is a BROKEN PROMISE and traps here, key in
// hand: the sought key's interned offset goes to scratch word 0 (the
// postmortem window), then a deterministic out-of-bounds load traps — the
// backtrace names the perform site, the scratch word the effect. The walk
// itself is the preamble's $ev_declaring_node, emitted beside its dual
// $world_push (backends/wasm.mn) — one mechanism, one home.

